Feature: Playing Towers with ArduPilot drones
  The game server plays Towers with real ArduPilot drones. Each drone is a T1-S VTOL with an
  ArduPlane [[SITL]] of its own, with the AGENT mode. The server flies each drone on the flight
  model of the sim, and it is the referee.

  At GO, each drone arms at its base and climbs in QHOVER. At 15 m it changes to FBWA, and then
  the server switches it to [[AGENT mode]]. From then on, the server sends each drone the plan
  for its order about once each second. To take a tower, a drone circles it 40 m out, inside its
  circle.

  Each team has a [[seat]]. A computer commander or a person at the commander app sits in it.
  A seat gets only the map of its team, four times each second. Thus the fog is the same for a
  person and for a computer. Other seats can watch a team, or the whole game.

  Expectation: Every drone flies the game in AGENT mode
    When two computer teams play a game of Towers
    Then the drones in AGENT mode are 10
    And the commands that the drones refused are 0

  Expectation: Each computer team takes towers
    When two computer teams play a game of Towers
    Then the towers that Blue took are at least 1
    And the towers that Red took are at least 1

  Expectation: A seat gets only what its team sees
    When two computer teams play a game of Towers
    Then the sightings from the fog on the Blue seat are 0
    And the Red drones that the Blue seat saw are at least 1

  Expectation: The ground reaches every drone through the air
    The ground does not talk to a drone's flight controller directly. Each frame goes through the
    simulated air, which drops it if the radio does not reach the drone: a drone in a radio
    shadow hears nothing from the ground and the ground hears nothing from it.

    When two computer teams play a game of Towers
    Then the frames that the air carried between the ground and the drones are at least 1

  Expectation: A drone tells the ground what it sees
    A drone has a sensor. When an enemy drone is in its sensor range, the drone reports it in its
    telemetry, as an ADSB_VEHICLE message, a second after a second. A report goes down the air like
    any other frame: a drone in a radio shadow reports nothing to its team.

    When two computer teams play a game of Towers
    Then the sensor reports that the drones sent are at least 1

  Expectation: No drone crashes in a game
    When two computer teams play a game of Towers
    Then the drones that crashed are 0
